  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Research proposal: investigate whether deliberately increasing Mn content in Fe80-xMnxCo10Cr10 alloys during directed energy deposition destabilizes the primary fcc phase and triggers in-situ phase transformations that interrupt columnar growth, enabling crack-free refined microstructures, using thermodynamic modeling, operando X-ray diffraction, and multiscale characterization.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Together, the retained and repaired conclusions motivate a study that links alloy composition, phase stability, AM processing, and microstructural refinement mechanisms."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Secondary phases can change microstructure in part by pinning grain boundaries, which can contribute to grain-refinement strategies."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Secondary-phase strategies must be considered cautiously because brittle intermetallic phases can cause significant cracking during solidification."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) The risk of cracking from brittle intermetallics, together with limited understanding of phase-transformation grain refinement, motivates further mechanism understanding."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Mechanism understanding should include phase stability, because phase stability-related energy differences affect strength and ductility."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Increasing Mn content is a relevant way to manipulate phase stability because it destabilizes the primary fcc phase during solidification."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) Fe80-xMnxCo10Cr10 MPEAs provide the alloy system used to study Mn-content effects on fcc-phase stability in an AM context."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) The study selects Fe80-xMnxCo10Cr10 variants referred to by Mn content, including Mn40, Mn45, and Mn50."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) The selected Mn-content variants are targeted compositions through which the study can evaluate whether composition affects the strength and performance of printed components."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) Understanding relationships among targeted composition, processing, and microstructure may help achieve crack-free, refined microstructures in AM."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) The study aims to illuminate a grain-refinement mechanism relevant to achieving crack-free, refined AM microstructures."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) MPEAs comprising 3d transition metals are widely explored examples within the broader class of complex high-performance MPEAs."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Additive manufacturing is presented as a compelling avenue for fabricating high-performance components from MPEAs, including widely explored 3d-transition-metal MPEA systems."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Processing MPEAs by additive manufacturing can improve strength relative to conventional methods, supporting AM as a relevant processing route rather than proving universal property enhancement."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) AM processing of MPEAs is motivated by potential strength benefits while also presenting processing challenges."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Because columnar grains produce highly textured microstructures and composition can alter AM microstructures, composition control is a plausible route to address texture-related microstructural concerns."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Composition design is a viable strategy to investigate for grain refinement in AM, particularly where textured microstructures motivate microstructural control."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Within composition-design approaches for AM grain refinement, introducing a secondary phase is one effective method for changing microstructure."];
